  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA slightly drier northwesterly flow has mixed downward to the\nsurface this afternoon, allowing a fair amount of sunshine to\nprevail across CNY/NEPA. This will lead into a mainly clear\nnight. Good radiating conditions and locally wet soils from\nrecent rainfall will lead to patchy fog development overnight\ninto early Friday across the twin tiers. \n\nA fast moving short-wave in the northern stream is still\nexpected to cross the region coincident with peak heating Friday\nafternoon. Despite fairly shallow moisture depths at that point,\nwe could see some isolated convection tied to this feature\nFriday afternoon into the early evening. \n\nAfter another clear-partly cloudy night on Friday night, with\nthe potential for patchy fog again, Saturday looks like a\nstellar day, with plenty of sunshine, and fairly comfortable\ntemperatures/humidity levels for mid-August.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAs surface ridging moves off the east coast Saturday night, a\nsurface warm front will approach and move into the eastern Great\nLakes by early Sunday. Notable warm air advection forcing and \nmoisture inflow will lead to an increase in shower activity, \nwith perhaps some elevated thunder, later Saturday night into \nSunday morning.\n\nThe aforementioned warm front is expected to trudge across\nCNY/NEPA during the day Sunday, while a cold front also\ngradually approaches from the northwest. A moist air mass and\ndecent forced ascent from these features will lead to a high\nprobability of showers and thunderstorms to close out the\nweekend. \n\nMost model guidance suggests that the air mass could take on\npseudo-tropical characteristics within the warm sector on\nSunday, with precipitable water values perhaps nearing 2\",\nparticularly across NEPA. This raises some concern for heavy\nconvective rainfall, with rainfall rates of 1.5-2\"+/hour likely\nsupported in that type of regime. However, this far out, \npertinent and specific details, like thunderstorm \nlongevity/repeat development potential are not clear. This is a \nsituation we'll continue to monitor over the next couple of \ndays.\n\nDeep-layered shear would also marginally support stronger wind\ngust potential, if sufficient destabilization could occur \nwithin the open warm sector. This scenario also has a lot of \nuncertainty, though, with enough cloud debris perhaps present to\ntamp down instability. Once again, we'll monitor this potential\ninto the weekend, as the event approaches.",
